{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790342205,"nonce":"kWMXv+7WhXKTFcnmGitWsTUKWT8=","title":"Every reachable function is lowered up front and the whole lowered module lives for the run","message":"lower_program / lower_until_nothing_is_owed lower every reachable body before the first instruction runs, and compile_module_lazily hands one Arc\u003cHirModule\u003e to every FunctionEntry, loaded, current_module and the LLVM module_context (strong count 4,500 to 5,440), so no body is ever released. For a program that runs a small part of itself, almost all of this is memory for code that never executes.\n\nEvidence. NUC instrumented, api.lua (1547 lines; returns at line 6 without the T library): 681 bodies, 23,080 blocks, 167,165 instructions, 191,511 values, clone 146 MB; heap 224.9 MB at the lower_program bodies mark (the phase peak), 158.5 after compile; what runs is chunk$0 (4,132 instructions, 2.4 % of the module), 81 interpreted and 60 Cranelift functions. Mac main binary: api peaks at 346 MB RSS / 310 MB footprint for a program that prints one line. bitwise 32k instructions = 34 MB; nextvar 58k = 110 MB (52 with the capacity fix); locals lowered HIR 330 MB (MallocStackLogging, HirFunction::create_value 230).\n\nSavings: api about 140 MB; bitwise about 30; nextvar 50 to 100; locals up to 300.\n\nFix: lower a body at its first interpreter call from the typed function it keeps, as compile and optimisation already are per bead; give each entry an Arc\u003cHirFunction\u003e slot so a body with no remaining consumer can be released. Package MEM-lazy-lowering; coordinate with LO-load-lowering-cost and LO-load-interp-first, which own the joined-chunk path in the same files.","files":null}]}